 {"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-7", "title": "Usage errors print a literal backslash-n into the message, and subcommand usage output collides with its own exit line on one line", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-20 (dogfood run, discovered while correcting a row title).\nREPRO: boardctl update DF-BOARDCTL-6 --title x   (any flag the subcommand does not define)\nEVIDENCE (cat -A, so the escapes are visible):\n  flag provided but not defined: -title$\n  boardctl update <id> [--status complete] [--worktree PATH] ... [-C dir]\\nboardctl: flag provided but not defined: -title$\nThe usage string contains an escaped '\\n' that was never interpreted as a newline, so the usage text and the following error line are printed run-together on ONE physical line, and the user literally sees the characters backslash-n in the middle of the message. Expected: the usage text on its own line(s), then the error line (or the error first, then usage).\nIMPACT: small but it degrades every usage error in the CLI - the first thing a user sees when they mistype a flag is mojibake-ish output that makes the tool look unpolished, and the run-together line makes the real error harder to spot in a terminal.\nALSO MEASURED (not filed separately): 'boardctl <cmd> --help' exits 1 for every subcommand while bare 'help'/'--help' exits 0. docs/dogfood/diagnostics.md records this as deliberate, so it is only mentioned here for context.\nFIX DIRECTION: emit the usage block with a real newline (or two separate writes) and keep the error line separate.", "created_at": "2026-09-20T04:52:22Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-20T05:36:45Z", "worker_status": "pending", "detail": {"fingerprint": "4266a31cbf0345ff522fb898891e1ba4c135ae8ca526b0a4e385063e8d78cfd3"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 3, "foreman_note": "wave dispatch tick boardctl-2026-09-20-04-54-08", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-DF-BOARDCTL-7", "branch": "wt/DF-BOARDCTL-7", "commit_hash": "ac5442e", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "literal backslash-n fixed in 2 usage closures, 20-subtest output table; merged+pushed, verdict c1d71e7a", "sessions": ["20260920_001314_4e0e27"]}
 {"id": "BT-040", "title": "Cut v0.1.7: published v0.1.6 asset predates 8 user-facing fixes (doc/artifact drift reopened)", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "Premise proven live 2026-09-21: downloaded v0.1.6 asset boardctl_linux_amd64 (sha256 OK) reports vcs.revision=9f437bc == v0.1.6^{commit}, while origin/main is 20 commits ahead with 8 user-facing fixes: serve re-upload replacement (DF-2), folder-upload mkdir+flatten fix (DF-6), report parser colon-less UTC offsets (DF-3), velocity event-only completions (DF-4), burndown window key fix (DF-1), usage-error backslash-n fix (DF-7), test-load hygiene (BT-039). README:17 pins v0.1.6 so every documented install path ships stale binaries. Off-by-one answer 2068 (go-cli-release-published-binary-drift) covers the recipe. AC: (1) README Current-release pin + every /releases/download URL moved to v0.1.7; (2) cut publishes v0.1.7 from a CLEAN CLONE of the tag; (3) downloaded asset sha256-verified, version prints v0.1.7, go version -m vcs.revision == tag commit == origin/main, vcs.modified=false; (4) behavior probe on temp board proves at least one shipped fix vs the v0.1.6 asset; (5) CI green on the cut.", "created_at": "2026-09-21T01:34:50Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-21T01:54:10Z", "worker_status": "complete", "detail": {"fingerprint": "9fce44c81f9fc9cc85ff3947b5f98fd01638b99483fcbd39821f3da20cd4c949"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 3, "foreman_note": "Drift class reopened after feature wave exactly as tick-82 predicted; closed again. 8 user-facing fixes now on the documented install path.", "worktree": null, "branch": null, "commit_hash": "f17253b9619d106ddad3eac727dfecb438e5ae00", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "v0.1.7 cut: README pins moved, tag v0.1.7=f17253b==origin/main, CI release 35551898349 green, downloaded asset sha256-verified (vcs.revision==tag commit, modified=false), behavior probe proves DF-7 usage-newline fix vs v0.1.6 asset. Judge PASS .gitreins/history/2026-09-21/ecf335cf/", "sessions": null, "completed_at": "2026-09-21 01:55:00"}
 {"id": "BT-041", "title": "CLI contract: `<cmd> --help` exits 1 (the documented validation-failure code) on every subcommand, and `event` with no --type silently appends an audit event instead of refusing", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-21 (PM cycle; published v0.1.7 asset, fresh temp board, 13 subcommands probed):\n(a) `boardctl <cmd> --help` exits 1 for every subcommand (create/update/event/header/show/list/import/render/serve/init/validate/doctor/stats) — the flag package's ErrHelp path flows out as a plain error — while bare `help` and `--help` exit 0. Any wrapper that classifies exit 1 as \"validation failure\" (README:192 \"Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 validation failure, 2 usage/board-not-found\") reads a help request as a failed board validation.\n(b) `boardctl event` with NO --type is not refused: it appends {\"event_type\":\"audit\",\"timestamp\":...,\"actor\":\"foreman\"} and exits 0, while the subcommand's own usage line (cmd/boardctl/main.go:653) advertises `event --type audit [flags]` and `event --type bogus` IS refused (exit 1, vocabulary listed). A typo'd or omitted type silently writes an audit event — the append-only audit trail takes a fact nobody asked for, with no error.\nREPRO COMMANDS: for c in create update event header show list import render serve init validate doctor stats; do /tmp/asset/boardctl_linux_amd64 $c --help; echo $?; done  -> rc=1 for all; bare `help` -> rc=0.\n  $B -C . event ; echo $? -> rc=0 ; tail -1 events.jsonl -> \"event_type\": \"audit\".\nPRIOR RECORD, NEVER FILED: DF-BOARDCTL-7's reasoning (complete row) says \"ALSO MEASURED (not filed separately): 'boardctl <cmd> --help' exits 1 for every subcommand while bare 'help'/'--help' exits 0\" — half of this row is a known, deliberately-deferred observation that never got its own row; the `event --type` half is new.\nWHY A ROW AND NOT A ONE-LINE FIX: the fix is a decision (which exit code a help request is), then a test that pins it across the whole subcommand set — every subcommand's help currently collides with the documented failure code, so one command's local fix would leave the contract asymmetric.\nAC: (1) the decided exit code for `<cmd> --help` is pinned by a test table covering EVERY subcommand, and README:192's taxonomy sentence names it; (2) `event` with no --type either documents an explicit default in its usage line or is refused — usage text and behavior must agree, with a test for whichever is chosen; (3) the published v0.1.7 asset (or the next cut) is re-probed and shows the decided codes, quote the probe.", "created_at": "2026-09-21T13:07:08Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T12:05:53Z", "worker_status": "complete", "detail": {"fingerprint": "5ffed9163df3abd68729f64938fd9933186220b1074a5483d582d28246525362"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 2, "foreman_note": "wave tick boardctl-2026-09-24-10-58-10; judge verdict .gitreins/history/2026-09-24/db8c871f/verdict.json", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-BT-041", "branch": "wt/BT-041", "commit_hash": "f08d28e", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "Wave worker glm-5.3-flash@zai-glm: parseFlags intercepts flag.ErrHelp centrally; every <sub> --help (16 subs x {-h,--help}) exits 0 with usage on stdout (pinned table test); bare help/--help unchanged; event without --type refuses exit 1 with the allowed-vocabulary list and writes nothing (md5-verified); README exit-code contract updated. Merged a703f4b, judge PASS.", "sessions": ["20260924_060952_65f0e5"], "completed_at": "2026-09-24 07:04:40", "capability_tags": ["cli", "contract", "docs"]}
-{"id": "BT-042", "title": "DEPLOY RECURRENCE (BT-038 predicted): ~/.local/bin/boardctl is a dirty from-HEAD build (79a1012, lacks the DF-6 folder-upload fix, reports version dev) with no install target and no drift probe", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-21 (PM cycle; read-only probes on the repo + the installed binary). This is the RECURRENCE BT-038's own row predicted for itself.\nFACTS:\n1. ~/.local/bin/boardctl mtime 2026-09-20 01:08. `go version -m ~/.local/bin/boardctl` -> mod ...v0.1.7-0.20260920060733-79a10123addd+dirty, vcs.revision=79a10123addd, vcs.modified=true. It is a gitignored, dirty, from-HEAD build — a DIFFERENT class of artifact from the README's primary install path (a SHA256SUMS-verified release asset that reports v0.1.7 and vcs.modified=false).\n2. BT-038's own completion named the refresh as \"rebuilt+installed ~/.local/bin/boardctl from merged main (incl. BT-037 fix + DF-1/3/4/5/7)\", but 79a1012 is the DF-BOARDCTL-2 merge and is NOT an ancestor of the DF-BOARDCTL-6 folder-upload fix: `git merge-base --is-ancestor 6189838 79a10123addd` -> rc=1. The installed copy therefore lacks the newest user-facing fix it claims to carry, and carries none of BT-039.\n3. `~/.local/bin/boardctl version --json` -> {\"version\":\"dev\",\"build\":\"dev\"}: a source build cannot even name its own revision, so drift is undetectable from the tool itself; `go version -m` is the only probe.\n4. Nothing documents or verifies an install: no install target in the Makefile (targets: build/test/vet/fmt/fmt-check/version-check/vuln-check/release/clean) and `grep -n 'local/bin' README.md Makefile docs/ skills/` has no hits. BT-038's own reasoning pre-declared this exact failure: \"a defined deploy step so ~/.local/bin/boardctl is refreshed when boardctl merges (today it is manual and therefore silently stale)\".\n5. Impact stays contained today: the boardctl-foreman-ops skill instructs foremen to build and use ./bin/boardctl before board writes, so foreman board writes do not use the stale copy. The exposure is any other caller of the PATH binary (sibling projects boards, operator one-offs) running a build with neither the DF-6 knobs nor the BT-039 hygiene.\n6. Deliberately NOT re-done in this cycle: a manual re-copy of the binary. Two writers building and installing into a live board repo while its tick lane can build is how the dirty-mtime confusion (BT-039) recurs; the durable half is the verification + provenance step, which is this row.\nAC: (1) an install target or a documented install step that installs the RELEASED asset by tag and verifies sha256, so the deployed binary is reproducible from the tag and identifiable; (2) a stated verification probe (e.g. `boardctl version --json` expected value, or `go version -m`) in README/Development so drift is detectable at any time, not just during a release audit; (3) after the next release, a probe shows the deployed binary reporting the released tag with vcs.modified=false and a revision that is an ancestor of that tag — quote the probe output; (4) the next cycle that re-cuts a release notes whether the installed copy moved, so this class gets a signal instead of a rediscovery.", "created_at": "2026-09-21T13:07:08Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-21T13:07:08Z", "worker_status": "pending", "detail": {"fingerprint": "637955a0964ae5ed87a200d77bd0672ad6f13b57a2ff1a7a256398c5b864894a"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 2, "foreman_note": null, "worktree": null, "branch": null, "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "sessions": null, "completed_at": null, "capability_tags": ["deploy", "install", "stale"]}
