 {"id":"BT-062","title":"[P2] SPEC: coding-hermes-boardctl — author the OpenAPI 3.x contract muster consumes, plus the consumer wiring","status":"pending","priority":"P2","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-24T15:16:47.792197+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"CONTEXT — what \"connect over muster\" means here (read before scoping).\n\nmuster (github.com/wojons/muster) turns any OpenAPI 3.x spec into a typed CLI\n(openapi-cli), an MCP server (openapi-mcp), a daemon, and a Go library. A consumer\nattaches with one config block:\n\n  {\"mcpServers\": {\"<name>\": {\"command\": \"openapi-mcp\",\n                             \"args\": [\"--spec\", \"<url-or-path>\", \"--base-url\", \"<api>\"]}}}\n\n(muster README \"mcpServers\" + docs/integration-guide.md §2; verified 2026-09-24.)\n\nDESIGN AUTHORITY for every protocol row: muster/specs/PROTOCOLS.md — \"no protocol\nimplementation row may start before its adapter satisfies this spec's pipeline and\ncross-cutting contracts on paper\". The pipeline is discover/ingest -> protocol-neutral\noperation model -> SHARED command+MCP generation -> per-protocol transport executor.\nOpenAPI 3.x is the FIRST adapter, not the engine. muster's board carries PROTO-001..008.\n\n\"Support for people to connect to us over the muster protocol\" therefore means, for this\nproject: (1) publish a muster-consumable OpenAPI 3.x contract for our own service,\n(2) ship the consumer config + a human quickstart, (3) PROVE muster's own binaries\ningest it and drive the live service. Nothing here re-implements muster — we consume it.\n\n             THIS ROW IS ONE THIRD OF A CHAIN (scope -> spec -> build) filed together\n2026-09-24 at the operator's request. Do not start this row until its predecessor\nhas landed; the spec row must not invent a surface the scope row has not decided, and\nthe build row must not guess a contract the spec row has not frozen.\n\n             Spec work: the generation rule must be stated before the document is written — this repo's rule is\nthat the registry is the single source (see internal/describe and cmd/gendocs). A hand-written spec\nhere would be the exception to that rule and needs its reason recorded. Also: the spec must describe\nthe serve surface as it exists, not an aspirational one; if a route the consumer wants does not\nexist yet, that is a separate row, not a spec fiction.\n\n             DELIVERABLE — the contract and the wiring, frozen on this row:\n             1. the OpenAPI 3.x document muster will be pointed at (file or served route), with its\n                location named precisely and its generation rule stated (hand-written vs generated\n                from the route table — this repo's own doctrine decides which)\n             2. operationIds, request/response schemas, and the servers[] url that muster dereferences\n                (muster falls back to the spec's own servers[].url when --base-url is omitted; a\n                RELATIVE servers url cannot be dereferenced standalone — say which case applies)\n             3. the consumer config block + a three-line human quickstart (the CLI form:\n                openapi-cli <generated-verb>), both added to this repo's docs\n             4. the parity rule: what keeps the spec from drifting off the real routes, in the\n                same shape as this repo's existing drift checks\n\n             ACCEPTANCE: the spec parses in muster's own parser, the consumer block is copy-pasteable,\n             and the parity rule is expressed as an executable check (not a comment).","detail":{"fingerprint":"68b0a4fdbd88df23c0fdeae8c6dd532fa6a16c31ba539a675c04acca4717e295"},"complexity":2,"updated_at":"2026-09-24T15:16:47.792197+00:00","capability_tags":["+integration","+protocol","+interop"],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":null,"completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":["BT-061"],"worktree":null,"branch":null}
 {"id":"BT-063","title":"[P2] BUILD: coding-hermes-boardctl — implement the muster surface and prove it end to end against the live service","status":"pending","priority":"P2","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-24T15:16:47.800722+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"CONTEXT — what \"connect over muster\" means here (read before scoping).\n\nmuster (github.com/wojons/muster) turns any OpenAPI 3.x spec into a typed CLI\n(openapi-cli), an MCP server (openapi-mcp), a daemon, and a Go library. A consumer\nattaches with one config block:\n\n  {\"mcpServers\": {\"<name>\": {\"command\": \"openapi-mcp\",\n                             \"args\": [\"--spec\", \"<url-or-path>\", \"--base-url\", \"<api>\"]}}}\n\n(muster README \"mcpServers\" + docs/integration-guide.md §2; verified 2026-09-24.)\n\nDESIGN AUTHORITY for every protocol row: muster/specs/PROTOCOLS.md — \"no protocol\nimplementation row may start before its adapter satisfies this spec's pipeline and\ncross-cutting contracts on paper\". The pipeline is discover/ingest -> protocol-neutral\noperation model -> SHARED command+MCP generation -> per-protocol transport executor.\nOpenAPI 3.x is the FIRST adapter, not the engine. muster's board carries PROTO-001..008.\n\n\"Support for people to connect to us over the muster protocol\" therefore means, for this\nproject: (1) publish a muster-consumable OpenAPI 3.x contract for our own service,\n(2) ship the consumer config + a human quickstart, (3) PROVE muster's own binaries\ningest it and drive the live service. Nothing here re-implements muster — we consume it.\n\n             THIS ROW IS ONE THIRD OF A CHAIN (scope -> spec -> build) filed together\n2026-09-24 at the operator's request. Do not start this row until its predecessor\nhas landed; the spec row must not invent a surface the scope row has not decided, and\nthe build row must not guess a contract the spec row has not frozen.\n\n             Build work: the E2E should point muster at a real running `boardctl serve` on a fixture board and\nshow a generated tool returning real board rows — and, per the scope row's read-only call, show that\na mutation is NOT reachable as a tool (that negative case is the load-bearing assertion for this\nproject, not a nice-to-have).\n\n             DELIVERABLE — implementation plus a LIVE proof:\n             1. land the spec + parity check the spec row froze\n             2. build muster from source and demonstrate its OWN binaries against this service:\n                - `openapi-mcp --spec <ours>` → the typed tools are listed and at least one READ tool\n                  executes against the live service and returns real data\n                - `openapi-cli <generated-verb>` → a typed command executes and prints a real response\n                - the generated CLI must be driven through the documented invocation, not a bespoke\n                  shell script standing in for it\n             3. paste the raw output of both as this row's evidence, with the exact commands\n             4. name any operation muster generates that an agent should NOT be given, and how it is\n                withheld (per the scope row's boundary call)\n\n             ACCEPTANCE: a verifier who has never seen this repo can reproduce the two muster\n             binaries working against the live service from the row's own text. A spec that parses\n             but whose generated tools fail against the service is a FAIL, not a partial.\n\n             NOTE: muster is PRIVATE (github.com/wojons/muster) — the build row needs GOPRIVATE +\n             GitHub auth; record the working incantation in the row.","detail":{"fingerprint":"7c90e125b0f333c177d66cd8b1d36e03758d0cf2d4f1bc554ac51241a696095f"},"complexity":3,"updated_at":"2026-09-24T15:16:47.800722+00:00","capability_tags":["+integration","+protocol","+interop"],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":null,"completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":["BT-062"],"worktree":null,"branch":null}
 {"id":"DF-BOARDCTL-12","title":"P2 — README quickstart's `sha256sum -c --ignore-missing` fails verbatim on a bare download (asset must keep its release filename)","status":"complete","priority":"P2","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-24T18:50:00+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"OBSERVED 2026-09-24 (dogfood run 15, fresh Debian 13 bunker agent 2909fec1). The README install block downloads the binary as boardctl_linux_amd64 and runs sha256sum -c --ignore-missing — that works. But any user who saves the binary under a different name (curl -o bc, browser default name) hits 'sha256sum: SHA256SUMS: no file was verified' and no line names the cause; the README DOES explain the filename requirement in prose three paragraphs later ('The downloaded binary must keep its release filename'), but the failure gives no pointer back to it. Reproduced on the bunker: binary saved as bc, checksum step fails; renamed to boardctl_linux_amd64, passes. WHY IT MATTERS: the checksum step is the trust anchor of the zero-dep install path and its failure mode looks like a corrupt download, pushing users to skip verification. FIX DIRECTION: the SKIPPED-LINES-style evidence pattern the tool already uses — when sha256sum exits with 'no file was verified' the docs (or a tiny wrapper note under the install block) should quote the error and its cause side by side. Docs-only fix.","detail":{"fingerprint":"dg15-df-boardctl-12-checksum-filename"},"complexity":2,"updated_at":"2026-09-24T23:11:46+00:00","capability_tags":[],"worker_status":"complete","commit_hash":"9ad1e35","guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"GREEN","worker_summary":"Wave worker glm-5.3-flash@zai-glm: README quickstart recipe PROVEN LIVE against real v0.1.8 assets (curl+sha256sum -c --ignore-missing exit 0, ./boardctl version v0.1.8; 7 published assets listed) — recipe correct as written, no defect; pinned by TestDF12QuickstartBlockPinned (block shape, tag agreement via versioncheck.DownloadURLRe, asset-naming rule, retired spellings 